# The pruner. Its absence cost two days of soundings.
[Unit]
Description=chirpsounder2 ringbuffer pruner (drf ringbuffer)
PartOf=chirp.target
# After the recorder, which creates the directory. `drf ringbuffer` watches a
# path rather than owning it, so starting first would leave it watching
# nothing until the next inotify event.
After=chirp-rx.service
Before=chirp-detect.service chirp-ionograms.service

[Service]
Type=simple
User=ionouser
WorkingDirectory=/home/ionouser/chirpsounder2

# Nothing else deletes ringbuffer data. Every example under
# examples/ringbuffer/ starts this process and stop_ringbuffer.sh already
# expects to kill it -- but no launcher started it, so /dev/shm reached 100%,
# the writer could no longer allocate, and the recording developed holes that
# surfaced downstream as "missing data - skipping" while the recorder, the
# detector and every plot carried on looking healthy.
#
# **The size buys time, not space.** It is how far back a consumer can reach,
# which is what decides whether a sounding is analysable at all:
#
#     seconds of history = size / (sample_rate * 4 bytes)
#
# At 25 MS/s that is 100 MB/s, so 12000MB holds 120 s and 14000MB holds 140 s.
# That arithmetic is now confirmed rather than assumed: calc_ionograms prints a
# `Buffer extent ...` line whose width measured 119 s at 12000MB and 139 s at
# 14000MB (DOB, 2026-08-12). An earlier version of this comment doubted it,
# because find_timings.py margins routinely exceed 120 s -- so it is "N s left"
# that is not measured from this window, and the formula that is right.
#
# The consequence is a hard ceiling on sweep length. A consumer running at
# fraction r of realtime is losing history at (1-r) per second, so the longest
# sweep it can finish is
#
#     max sweep = r * B / (1 - r)
#
# At DOB's r = 0.66 and B = 119 s that is 231 s -- which is why Cyprus, whose
# sweep is 250 s, wrote nothing at 12000MB and completes at 14000MB. Reach for
# this formula before reaching for RAM: raising r is usually cheaper, and at
# r = 0.68 the same 12000MB would have carried Cyprus.
#
# Watch swap after any increase. The tmpfs pages are not reclaimable, so the
# size comes straight out of what the OOM killer sees as free; if swap grows,
# the buffer cost more than it bought. See OOMScoreAdjust in chirp-rx.service.
#
# **Match the minus sign, and read the whole log.** Two ways to get this wrong,
# both of which happened on DOB 2026-08-11:
#
#   * `grep -o '[0-9.]* s left'` silently drops the sign, so `-5.2 s left` is
#     counted as a comfortable 5.2. Every failure reads as a pass. Use
#     `grep -oE '\-?[0-9.]+ s left'`.
#   * A `tail -20` gave min 90.6 s and "none under 60", which reads as healthy.
#     The full log said otherwise.
#
# Measured correctly over 1333 soundings: **57 negative (4.28%)**, 6.3% under
# 10 s, 13.1% under 30 s. The 57 match the `missing data - skipping` lines in
# ionograms.log one for one -- that is data lost, not close calls, and it is
# lost silently. A margin under 30 s is a near miss; only <= 0 is a loss.
#
#     grep -oE '\-?[0-9.]+ s left' logs/find_timings.log \
#       | awk '{n++; if($1<=0) z++} END {printf "n=%d lost=%d (%.2f%%)\n", n, z+0, 100*z/n}'
#
# Storage under the consumer matters as much as this buffer's size. DOB lost
# 4.28% while writing products to a 5400 rpm laptop disk over ntfs-3g -- FUSE,
# userspace, seek-bound -- with a mirror job reading the same spindle every
# five minutes. `ionice` does not reach the ntfs-3g daemon that does that I/O.
#
# Raise the size on a machine with more RAM. **Do not move this to an SSD.**
# An earlier version of this comment recommended exactly that, and the
# arithmetic says otherwise: 25 MS/s of complex int16 is 100 MB/s, which is
# 8.64 TB written *per day*. A 1 TB consumer SSD rated ~600 TBW is exhausted
# in 69 days; a write-intensive enterprise drive at 3 DWPD lasts under two
# years. tmpfs has no write endurance to spend, which is why a ring buffer
# belongs in RAM and why the only real lever here is more of it.
#
# The SSD advice is right for the *products* -- 0.6 MB per ionogram, a few
# thousand a day -- just never for the stream.
#
# Before buying RAM, check the size would help. The margin that matters is how
# far *below* zero the failures fall: another 120 s of history saves a sounding
# that missed by 5 s and does nothing for one that missed by 200.
#
#     grep -oE '\-?[0-9.]+ s left' logs/find_timings.log | awk '$1<=0' \
#       | awk '{n++; if($1>-120) s++} END {printf "%d of %d saved by +120s\n", s+0, n}'
ExecStart=/home/ionouser/chirpsounder2/.venv38/bin/drf ringbuffer \
    -z 14000MB /dev/shm/hf25 -p 2

Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10

[Install]
WantedBy=chirp.target
